<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Saybrook Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1635 CE – 1644 CE · capital Saybrook</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived English fort colony at the mouth of the Connecticut River, planned as a refuge for Puritan lords and sold to Connecticut in 1644.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1632 a group of Puritan aristocrats, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele">Viscount Saye and Sele</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-greville-2nd-baron-brooke">Lord Brooke</a>, obtained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-patent">Warwick Patent</a> to land in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. In 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop-the-younger">John Winthrop the Younger</a>, acting as their governor, sent men to build a fort at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, named Saybrook after the two patrons.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gardiner">Lion Gardiner</a> built and commanded the fort, which was meant to guard the river and prepare a home for the lords and their followers, who never came. The settlement remained a small garrison with a few families. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a> it was besieged by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pequot-nation">Pequot Nation</a> in 1636 to 1637 and served as a base for English expeditions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-fenwick">George Fenwick</a>, one of the patentees, later lived there as governor.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> keeping the patrons at home, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-fenwick">Fenwick</a> sold the fort and its land to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/connecticut-colony">Connecticut Colony</a> in 1644. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-saybrook">Old Saybrook</a> preserves the name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saybrook-colony.jpg" alt="Saybrook Colony" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctcolony.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1635 CE – 1644 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saybrook</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Winthrop the Younger, Lion Gardiner, George Fenwick</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Puritanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saybrook</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=saybrook-colony" aria-label="Saybrook Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2913 705 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Saybrook Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3233 961L3231 946"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3231 946h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3233 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3233 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=saybrook-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
